  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAn upper level low will pass over the region today bringing\nadditional scattered showers to the region. We will also start a\ncooling trend given the change to a northwesterly wind pattern.\nSome lake moisture may get pulled into the picture later today\ninto Sunday keeping the possibility for a few showers around.\nTemperatures should generally be in the 40's and 50's through\nMonday. A shortwave passing through the area Monday should\nrenew the potential for some showers likely aided by moisture \noff of the northern most Great Lake. A brief break in the action\nis possible Tuesday and Wednesday. Clearing may be slower than \nforecasted on Tuesday given typical NBM biases. Winds look to be\ngusty most days with modeled momentum transfer showing the \npotential for 20 mph or higher gusts at times. \n\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nHere is where things have been trending more interesting and\npossibly impactful over the past couple of days. The previous \nshortwaves should start of the process of sinking the baroclinic \ngradient further south allowing some colder air to gradually \nfilter south later next week. A fairly potent clipper type \nsystem is modeled to dive into the plains and Ohio Valley by \nThursday then complete a Miller B type transfer along the coast \nFriday. Where and how that happens features quite a bit of \nensemble spread at this point and a high degree of uncertainity \nwith most weather elements. The NAO looks to trend favorable \nfrom a modest negative state to slightly positive which would \nsupport the Nor'easter potential. For now, rain chances have \nincreased some Thursday through Friday and temperatures do \nsuggest some wintry potential as well. If the track is to our \nsouth, dynamical cooling may allow for a change to a period of \nslushy snow. Higher elevations would be favored for any mix.",
